[[:db/retract 17592186122678 :answer-field/choices 17592186122679] [:db/retract 17592186122678 :answer-field/choices 17592186122680] [:db/retract 17592186122678 :answer-field/choices 17592186122681] [:db/retract 17592186122678 :answer-field/choices 17592186122682] [:db/retract 17592186122678 :answer-field/choices 17592186122683] {:db/id 17592186124522 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2ffb7e93-f33c-5adf-acf0-f55e6fbbb059"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124331 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda$ of the matrix \n![](images/45/4549ab32dddcf46e297a3238d71a51657bbcf5fa1c4b2659000c023fff6f938d.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/99/99bf36b6acbdd21ab16ca6b434122fed7c474103f6e88ec5688261d5848d542c.svg)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(A-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-9-\\lambda  & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda  & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda )(6-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +9)(\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-9,$ $\\lambda_2=9,$ and $\\lambda_3=6.$ \n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124380 :question/problem "![](images/2e/2e17e4fa743f6f8d3f5987b9095cf6976ae8eadf36ee4456ea8a9195201526e4.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda=4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nII. $\\lambda=-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda=6$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\, P-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/38/38316eff018e61b4f9f6ce90e0e231ed1eeb6fe993f3b107a5ad8057e3641fd4.svg)\n\nThe characteristic equation is $\\det(P-\\lambda I) = 0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4 \\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 0 & -8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3 \\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-\\lambda )\\cdot (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 7 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )((-1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-35) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-36) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda=3,$ $\\lambda=4,$ $\\lambda=6,$ and $\\lambda=-6$ (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124332 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues ![](images/98/98a445be63a324ba80515c96b7f891797392bdeafb375acb75ce1613f4475d4c.svg) of the matrix \n![](images/f1/f1bc09a8e52ccd286aa0e28fee7f60b17b48ce7423eb897269ab9fda387a93dc.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix ![](images/da/da1b043be21ae49536ac74a7c5df40af321f327b25fce325a54d87bca7bdd804.svg)\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/0c/0ced5cb154bbec90b1d0832ac1e2bf9e0b3599d4675784a47b32f69861b63549.svg)\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by ![](images/7d/7db7ee38ff2ad420ad85266b4684a9092ca4976b0a312043cdcb40f755f87312.svg) \n\nExpanding the determinant across the ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg)st row, we get the following:\n\n![](images/8a/8a07ee34efc93d9c0454d7275b94f5dcd303a938784b797a265eba29356f5801.svg)\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of ![](images/22/225eed5110c60c0ca4c2205a24ba280024533de3e65ad4d32fae9617ce06b4a9.svg) \n![](images/04/04cd74c64ddaedf3b73761d8098979ac86cd9089751a10278b9aedd2bc8f98dc.svg)\n\nLet's pick ![](images/96/9656fb92a4f17a34456f345565f0e7d85fd432d9cd38070cdada71e52647529c.svg) Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n![](images/5d/5d2e25abee3cc05da3b3a83482899c4b6558fd54a4d66e39331ba1b55f50a917.svg)\nSo, ![](images/0a/0a68ef90ceec4971dc74a1a73e0a5bbdee3eec5956ddbce404a48517b2828059.svg) is a root of the equation. This means we can factor ![](images/ff/ffad613e6c3731c1406a83b447c4212d48bc41005ca7817a73d5c2ed66f79b30.svg) out of ![](images/03/0367fad967f1c2089ceaefb337b32b0a39b425dd3f0f900ec7b859547a036a89.svg) using synthetic division:\n\n| ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/0c/0cf2eee0cdec31b7acdc244a255aaee7d6d520cfeee67b64ac9cf215f5377477.svg) | ![](images/f1/f1557a7d98a910797c6117a185f64b753b0ebcd56db3f187aca1952f4f38df5c.svg) | ![](images/8e/8ec9094ad8c9b7c6e3da424099eca8e856a690c4355d4af8210da28f495a72f0.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ![](images/11/113a2e06675ede52bfef4b9178bf4e7820332c07c597cb963b36a5bbef55f6cf.svg) | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) | ![](images/46/46908fb7561d19927fe58a38ab26cba3ff8a35cf75cc0da234f08773dc991398.svg) |\n|  | ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg) | ![](images/37/37e17c4a2302e6352fb7186621c9c43bb5e99d43a8a0bc700c0181d36c6cc915.svg) | ![](images/46/46908fb7561d19927fe58a38ab26cba3ff8a35cf75cc0da234f08773dc991398.svg) | ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n![](images/d8/d802c5d6ff81a500a8d7d00ec9e20daafb07d355a1ce0d9a0b48dc03a88088c9.svg)\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ![](images/19/191f231d4e62c28931ed18cf133acb0e0ec66e845af743409198c4420953bd52.svg) ![](images/eb/eb3981cb149c9d1b26c2b87bd92a5aef5ead1787d8cb817fa3c1efd6a66074f9.svg) and ![](images/e9/e988f1ab98136f2d888e83baf436d8f659654a55eb2d2b3e11029b365a52c6e0.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ffb7e93-f33c-5adf-acf0-f55e6fbbb059" :question/id #uuid "2ffb7e93-f33c-5adf-acf0-f55e6fbbb059" :question/math-academy-id "q-36831" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,B-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nB-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(B-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nSince the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the entries on the main diagonal, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are always the entries on the main diagonal." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0da5b7ce-3185-5f7f-9a0f-797454d122c3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0da5b7ce-3185-5f7f-9a0f-797454d122c3" :answer-field/id #uuid "0da5b7ce-3185-5f7f-9a0f-797454d122c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0fde5085-a635-5696-9cd7-100b3c72cad2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-578c36b5-c09b-5821-8e36-ba81ab9528bc" "answer-ed7b9e7c-c2fe-5559-be97-bb8125d6fa51" "answer-0fde5085-a635-5696-9cd7-100b3c72cad2" "answer-78e32da7-f1e3-50b7-a080-2498d2b37830" "answer-cfdc823e-90d7-5645-a8fc-5def08fa694a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-578c36b5-c09b-5821-8e36-ba81ab9528bc" :answer/id #uuid "578c36b5-c09b-5821-8e36-ba81ab9528bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,-4,1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed7b9e7c-c2fe-5559-be97-bb8125d6fa51" :answer/id #uuid "ed7b9e7c-c2fe-5559-be97-bb8125d6fa51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,2,0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fde5085-a635-5696-9cd7-100b3c72cad2" :answer/id #uuid "0fde5085-a635-5696-9cd7-100b3c72cad2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{-1,4,1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78e32da7-f1e3-50b7-a080-2498d2b37830" :answer/id #uuid "78e32da7-f1e3-50b7-a080-2498d2b37830" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{2,0,1\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfdc823e-90d7-5645-a8fc-5def08fa694a" :answer/id #uuid "cfdc823e-90d7-5645-a8fc-5def08fa694a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\lambda \\in \\{2,4,0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366365 :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nII. $\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366006 :question/problem "$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $C$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n\nII. $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$\n\nIII. The eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125013 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda |\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|+(-3)|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3-\\lambda  \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ -\\lambda ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3)+2(2\\lambda -2)-3(2\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda )+(4\\lambda -4)-(6\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122678 :answer-field/correct "answer-ba3bf449-9417-55d8-bda3-bcdf2a3abb0f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2dc50313-1349-57e5-bd5b-c32ef21846c4" "answer-ba3bf449-9417-55d8-bda3-bcdf2a3abb0f" "answer-f431214a-fe2c-58fb-acc6-9d70412cfbb8" "answer-c9d9c2bc-05e4-5243-ab8a-17562b551bd5" "answer-bb4847f7-518c-5520-9f7b-8f055c77b9d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dc50313-1349-57e5-bd5b-c32ef21846c4" :answer/id #uuid "2dc50313-1349-57e5-bd5b-c32ef21846c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -4=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba3bf449-9417-55d8-bda3-bcdf2a3abb0f" :answer/id #uuid "ba3bf449-9417-55d8-bda3-bcdf2a3abb0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f431214a-fe2c-58fb-acc6-9d70412cfbb8" :answer/id #uuid "f431214a-fe2c-58fb-acc6-9d70412cfbb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-2\\lambda -2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9d9c2bc-05e4-5243-ab8a-17562b551bd5" :answer/id #uuid "c9d9c2bc-05e4-5243-ab8a-17562b551bd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -2=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb4847f7-518c-5520-9f7b-8f055c77b9d8" :answer/id #uuid "bb4847f7-518c-5520-9f7b-8f055c77b9d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{3}-5{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda =0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125785 :tutorial/content "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/21/21e0994519fbba32a84020ce4661e98600c1307947083ddc3afe9902658143c1.svg)\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124749 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
